response:
 You are correct in your MATLAB code to find the solutions for "a", "b", and "c" given the system of equations you provided. Once you have the values for "a", "b", and "c" from the "sol" variable, you can substitute them into the equation for "x" (x = a + 2b - c/3) to find its value. Here's how you can do it:
```MATLAB
% Given system of equations
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;
% Solve for [a, b, c]
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);
% Substitute the values of [a, b, c] into the equation for x
[a_sol, b_sol, c_sol] = sol.values; % Get the numerical values of [a, b, c]
x_value = a_sol + 2*b_sol - c_sol/3;
```
This code will give you the value of "x" based on the given system of equations.

