 To make the circle element look more 3D, you can add some additional CSS properties. Here's an example of how you could modify the `.circle` class to give it a more pronounced 3D effect:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) drop-shadow(-15px -15px 15px gray); /* Add a negative shadow for depth */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75), -10px -10px 5px 0px rgba(255,255,255,0.75); /* Add a white shadow for highlight */
  border-radius: 50%; /* Ensure a perfect circle */
  width: 100px; /* Set a fixed width for consistent dimensions */
  height: 100px;
  position: relative;
  transform-style: preserve-3d; /* Allow 3D transforms */
}

.circle:hover {
  transform: rotateX(15deg) rotateY(15deg); /* Add a slight rotation on hover for interaction */
}
```

This updated CSS adds a negative drop shadow to create depth and a positive white shadow to simulate highlights, giving the circle a more prominent 3D appearance. Additionally, we set a fixed width and height, positioned the circle relatively, enabled 3D transforms, and added a slight rotation on hover. Remember that this is just one way to achieve a 3D effect, and you might need to adjust these values based on your specific design requirements.

