 To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using a Chrome Extension, follow these steps to configure your boilerplate files:

1. Manifest File (manifest.json):
   The manifest file is crucial for defining essential information about your Chrome Extension. You need to add the following keys and values in your `manifest.json` file to enable content scripts.

   ```json
   {
     "manifest_version": 2,
     "name": "My Chrome Extension",
     "description": "Description of My Chrome Extension",
     "version": "1.0",
     "content_scripts": [
       {
         "matches": ["<URL_OF_THE_PAGE>/*"], // Replace with the URL you want to target
         "js": ["contentScript.js"] // Replace with the name of your content script file
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

2. Content Script File (contentScript.js):
   Create a new file called `contentScript.js` (or whatever name you used in the manifest file) inside your project directory. This file will contain the code that runs on the targeted webpage.

   For example, if you want to modify the page's title, you can use the following code snippet as a starting point:

   ```javascript
   document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
     // Your code here
     document.title = 'New Title';
   });
   ```

3. Load the Extension in Chrome:
   Open Google Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions`. Enable "Developer mode" by clicking the toggle switch at the top right corner. Click "Load unpacked" and select the folder containing your Chrome Extension files.

Now, your Chrome Extension should be injecting the specified JavaScript code into the targeted webpage whenever it loads. Remember that the content script runs in a sandbox environment, so make sure to follow security best practices when writing your code.

